martinmunk-avr-controld (0.4.1) stable; urgency=medium

  * feed_demand: when a source demand clears while the receiver stays on for
    another demand, restore that demand's input (e.g. stop AirPlay to Main while
    the TV is on -> switch Main back to the TV input).
  * Packaging: start/restart the service on install and upgrade (was left stopped
    after apt upgrade). The unit is gated on ConditionPathExists=the config, so a
    fresh install without config does not crash-loop.

 -- Martin Munk Hansen <skiers.56.haggis@icloud.com>  Wed, 16 Sep 2026 13:10:00 +0000

martinmunk-avr-controld (0.4.0) stable; urgency=medium

  * First release of the Python avr-controld: a broker daemon that owns each AV
    receiver connection and serves the ALSA plugins and avr-cec-relay over the
    same Unix-socket protocol as the C++ daemon, plus a Home Assistant MQTT
    bridge. Shipped as a bundled virtualenv (arch: all; pure-Python deps).
  * NAD backend (RS232, T7xx) exposing power/volume/mute/source plus extras
    (bass/treble, Zone 2 power/volume/mute/source, sleep, tone, model/firmware).
  * Multi-zone AirPlay: one shairport-sync watcher per zone over per-zone D-Bus
    (zones= config), each mapped to a receiver zone (Main core, or Zone 2 via
    extras). Phone slider -> zone volume (clamped to a per-zone ceiling) and
    reverse (receiver remote/front panel -> phone slider via SetAirplayVolume).
  * Power policy per demand (AirPlay playing, CEC/TV active) with off-grace;
    per-zone power grace; drop the AirPlay session when the receiver input is
    switched away from a zone. Demands act on activation edges so a client may
    re-assert them (survives a daemon restart).
  * Serial access is finely locked so a long state sweep never blocks interactive
    commands (CEC/phone volume); volume-signal bursts are coalesced.
  * Offline unit tests (no hardware/MQTT/serial) run hermetically in CI.

 -- Martin Munk Hansen <skiers.56.haggis@icloud.com>  Wed, 16 Sep 2026 11:35:00 +0000
